75
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:56,800
Music.

76
00:04:56,800 --> 00:04:59,800
So I have a couple of messages from Buss Price,

77
00:04:59,800 --> 00:05:05,800
who's the Scoutmaster of Troop 349 in Verona, Wisconsin.

78
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:08,800
First one has to do with planning,

79
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:11,800
And Buss writes. I just listened to your podcast

80
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:13,800
on leadership, which I could have listened to this.

81
00:05:13,800 --> 00:05:15,800
three years ago,

82
00:05:15,800 --> 00:05:17,800
We went through the blank stares stages

83
00:05:17,800 --> 00:05:20,800
of event planning for multiple cycles,

84
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:23,800
Even ended up with plans that didn't really work-

85
00:05:23,800 --> 00:05:25,800
canoe trips on the calendar during cold months,

86
00:05:25,800 --> 00:05:26,800
for example.

87
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:28,800
Finally, someone- it was probably you

88
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:30,800
in one of your podcasts- pointed out

89
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:33,800
that we had fewer choices when we were youth.

90
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:35,800
So we ended up creating a framework

91
00:05:35,800 --> 00:05:37,800
for the Scouts to plan their events-

92
00:05:37,800 --> 00:05:40,800
Big events listed by categories such as canoeing,

93
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:42,800
hiking, biking, camping, spelunking.

94
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:45,800
Each of those areas had two or three choices

95
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:48,800
of location and some target months

96
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:50,800
of when the weather would best work out for them.

97
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:53,800
This framework made our annual planning conference

98
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:54,800
enjoyable.

99
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:56,800
Well, Buss, I think what happened somewhere along

100
00:05:56,800 --> 00:05:59,800
the way was someone decided they would

101
00:05:59,800 --> 00:06:01,800
try to apply an administrative fix

102
00:06:01,800 --> 00:06:03,800
to the long-standing problem.

103
00:06:03,800 --> 00:06:06,800
Many Scout Troops have a poor record of camping

104
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:08,800
and really go back as far as you want

105
00:06:08,800 --> 00:06:10,800
in Scouting Literature and you'll find

106
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:12,800
this is a perennial problem.

107
00:06:12,800 --> 00:06:15,800
There are a lot of Troops that, for whatever reason,

108
00:06:15,800 --> 00:06:17,800
don't end up getting out camping very much.

109
00:06:17,800 --> 00:06:20,800
So my guess is that the whole idea

110
00:06:20,800 --> 00:06:22,800
of an annual planning conference

111
00:06:22,800 --> 00:06:24,800
was seen as one way to encourage troops

112
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:27,800
to actually set a calendar and make plans

113
00:06:27,800 --> 00:06:30,800
and long-range plans to get out and go camping.

114
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:33,800
Now, if adults sit down and they make those plans,

115
00:06:33,800 --> 00:06:35,800
well you know the Scouts may find

116
00:06:35,800 --> 00:06:37,800
that they're interested in those activities or not.

117
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:41,800
So including youth leadership into the plan

118
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:43,800
and getting them to buy in

119
00:06:43,800 --> 00:06:47,800
and to create as much of the plan as possible

120
00:06:47,800 --> 00:06:50,800
is important, not only because they would buy in

121
00:06:50,800 --> 00:06:52,800
to the ideas that they came up with.

122
00:06:52,800 --> 00:06:56,800
but that's a good process for them to go through.

123
00:06:56,800 --> 00:06:59,800
So making the choices that come out

124
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:03,800
of an annual planning conference democratic,

125
00:07:03,800 --> 00:07:06,800
that's kind of a next step type of thing. right,

126
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:08,800
If we're going to have this conference

127
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:10,800
and the Scouts are supposed to have a part in it,

128
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:12,800
well, we'll make it a democratic thing.

129
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:14,800
Everybody will vote on what we're going to do.

130
00:07:14,800 --> 00:07:18,800
So, with all of those elements combined,

131
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:23,800
now we have what we call the annual planning conference.

132
00:07:23,800 --> 00:07:25,800
It's a highly touted thing.

133
00:07:25,800 --> 00:07:27,800
It's something that is kind of like

134
00:07:27,800 --> 00:07:30,800
an expectation that's baked into the BSA program.

135
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:32,800
But I'll tell you this:

136
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:37,800
I stopped doing annual planning conferences in that way

137
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:39,800
a long time ago.

138
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:42,800
Now. when you hear that, it may sound like that

139
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:44,800
running its counter to my general thing

140
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:46,800
about having real responsibility

141
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:49,800
and being able to make real decisions and that.

142
00:07:49,800 --> 00:07:51,800
But what we've done, I fear,

143
00:07:51,800 --> 00:07:55,800
is we've set way too high a premium on planning.

144
00:07:55,800 --> 00:07:59,800
I hear about this sort of thing on a regular basis.

145
00:07:59,800 --> 00:08:01,800
You know, planning conferences are hard

146
00:08:01,800 --> 00:08:03,800
because the Scouts don't come up with ideas

147
00:08:03,800 --> 00:08:05,800
and they're very reluctant to do that,

148
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:07,800
And then when they're given the ability to do that,

149
00:08:07,800 --> 00:08:09,800
yeah, they're not real excited about it

150
00:08:09,800 --> 00:08:11,800
and it takes them a long time

151
00:08:11,800 --> 00:08:13,800
And they come up with poor plans

152
00:08:13,800 --> 00:08:15,800
that we don't like very much.

153
00:08:15,800 --> 00:08:18,800
So how do you work your way through all of this?

154
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:20,800
Well, first of all, put the whole idea

155
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:23,800
of the annual planning conference aside for a moment.

156
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:25,800
You know we still have an annual meeting

157
00:08:25,800 --> 00:08:27,800
where we sit down and we look at our plans

158
00:08:27,800 --> 00:08:28,800
for the coming year.

159
00:08:28,800 --> 00:08:32,799
It's just that we don't necessarily have to invent

160
00:08:32,799 --> 00:08:35,799
a 12-month calendar at that meeting.

161
00:08:35,799 --> 00:08:39,799
If you were following along in recent past podcasts

162
00:08:39,799 --> 00:08:44,800
we presented the allegory of scouting as a game,

163
00:08:44,800 --> 00:08:46,800
and it's a pretty good allegory.

164
00:08:46,800 --> 00:08:48,800
It breaks down in some places,

165
00:08:48,800 --> 00:08:52,800
But if you think about it in those lines of logic,

166
00:08:52,800 --> 00:08:56,800
we play a specific game on a specific field of play.

167
00:08:56,800 --> 00:09:00,800
that means there's not an endless number of choices

168
00:09:00,800 --> 00:09:03,800
to make when we are planning our year.

169
00:09:03,800 --> 00:09:06,800
A basketball team doesn't get together

170
00:09:06,800 --> 00:09:08,800
for an annual planning conference to say,

171
00:09:08,800 --> 00:09:10,800
well, we're going to play these three games

172
00:09:10,800 --> 00:09:11,800
on a basketball court

173
00:09:11,800 --> 00:09:14,800
and those two will play on a football field.

174
00:09:14,800 --> 00:09:17,800
These three games we will play on a basketball court,

175
00:09:17,800 --> 00:09:19,800
but using the rules of baseball.

176
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:21,800
You understand what I'm saying.

177
00:09:21,800 --> 00:09:23,800
There is a frame of reference for scouting,

178
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:25,800
in the same way that there's a frame of reference

179
00:09:25,800 --> 00:09:26,800
for a sports team.

180
00:09:26,800 --> 00:09:29,800
So if you want to avoid getting kind of wrapped around

181
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:33,800
your own axle about this whole annual planning conundrum

182
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:35,800
and the whole idea of a patrol leader's council

183
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:37,800
sitting down and planning,

184
00:09:37,800 --> 00:09:41,800
remember that most of the plans are taken care of already.

185
00:09:41,800 --> 00:09:43,800
because what do we do?

186
00:09:43,800 --> 00:09:48,800
We go camping, and just going camping is more important

187
00:09:48,800 --> 00:09:50,800
than the location and the activity.

188
00:09:50,800 --> 00:09:53,800
Just going camping is more important

189
00:09:53,800 --> 00:09:55,800
than the location and the activity.

190
00:09:55,800 --> 00:09:58,800
If you have a basketball team that never plays a game,

191
00:09:58,800 --> 00:10:00,800
why?

192
00:10:00,800 --> 00:10:04,800
Or if you just want to cherry pick the best games

193
00:10:04,800 --> 00:10:05,800
out of your season,

194
00:10:05,800 --> 00:10:07,800
No, you have to keep playing the game

195
00:10:07,800 --> 00:10:09,800
for this whole thing to work.

196
00:10:09,800 --> 00:10:12,800
So go out camping as frequently as possible.

197
00:10:12,800 --> 00:10:16,800
Not every single camping trip is going to be a whiz bang.

198
00:10:16,800 --> 00:10:18,800
I'm having the best time of my life experience

199
00:10:18,800 --> 00:10:21,800
in the same way that a basketball game.

200
00:10:21,800 --> 00:10:24,800
Some are great, very energizing,

201
00:10:24,800 --> 00:10:25,800
Some are not,

202
00:10:25,800 --> 00:10:28,800
And some just are terrible. right,

203
00:10:28,800 --> 00:10:30,800
But you're playing the game.

204
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:34,800
My scouts typically chose the same thing

205
00:10:34,800 --> 00:10:35,800
over and over and over again

206
00:10:35,800 --> 00:10:38,800
From year to year. different sets of scouts,

207
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:41,800
15, 20 years apart, would choose the same activities.

208
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:44,800
And that was fine with me because, even though

209
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:47,800
I might be bored to tears after going to exactly

210
00:10:47,800 --> 00:10:51,800
the same camping spot for the 15th, the 20th,

211
00:10:51,800 --> 00:10:54,800
the 30th time. it was all new to them

212
00:10:54,800 --> 00:10:57,800
And we didn't have to go to all these hero locations

213
00:10:57,800 --> 00:11:01,800
Going 45 minutes up the road to the county park.

214
00:11:01,800 --> 00:11:02,800
it's a great spot,

215
00:11:02,800 --> 00:11:05,800
But what was most important is we were playing the game.

216
00:11:05,800 --> 00:11:08,800
So I said I had a couple of messages from Bus,

217
00:11:08,800 --> 00:11:11,800
and the second one has to do with patrols,

218
00:11:11,800 --> 00:11:14,800
And Bus said this: I've been having some issues

219
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:17,800
with patrols over the last three years.

220
00:11:17,800 --> 00:11:20,800
We formed patrols by having each scout list

221
00:11:20,800 --> 00:11:22,800
two or three scouts they wanted in their patrol

222
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:24,800
and this worked well.

223
00:11:24,800 --> 00:11:26,800
We had two years where we didn't end up

224
00:11:26,800 --> 00:11:29,800
recruiting many scouts and the patrols got smaller-

225
00:11:29,800 --> 00:11:31,800
Not enough scouts- and a patrol went camping

226
00:11:31,800 --> 00:11:33,800
to operate as a patrol.

227
00:11:33,800 --> 00:11:35,800
We tried your method of shuffling patrols

228
00:11:35,800 --> 00:11:37,800
each quarter and that took care of the issue

229
00:11:37,800 --> 00:11:39,800
for camping, because the new problem

230
00:11:39,800 --> 00:11:42,800
during meetings we have three or four older scouts

231
00:11:42,800 --> 00:11:44,800
migrate to their friends during the meeting

232
00:11:44,800 --> 00:11:47,800
and that kind of breaks up the whole patrol idea.

233
00:11:47,800 --> 00:11:50,800
So let me ask you, how do you deal with the few scouts

234
00:11:50,800 --> 00:11:52,800
who just won't operate in a patrol?

235
00:11:52,800 --> 00:11:54,800
They tend to focus on their own needs

236
00:11:54,800 --> 00:11:57,800
and not on the needs of their patrol.

237
00:11:57,800 --> 00:12:00,800
I think probably what most of us want

238
00:12:00,800 --> 00:12:03,800
is the idealized patrol that you would find

239
00:12:03,800 --> 00:12:06,800
in the 1940s era patrol leader's handbook.

240
00:12:06,800 --> 00:12:10,800
I still have the dog-eared copy of that book I had as a kid

241
00:12:10,800 --> 00:12:13,800
and it's illustrated with line drawings and cartoons

242
00:12:13,800 --> 00:12:17,800
of a bunch of cheery fellows doing things in an orderly fashion,

243
00:12:17,800 --> 00:12:21,800
and it depicts an idealized patrol. that never was,

244
00:12:21,800 --> 00:12:24,800
and I chased that idealized patrol for a long time

245
00:12:24,800 --> 00:12:27,800
and I may have seen glimpses of it now and then,

246
00:12:27,800 --> 00:12:31,800
but I never attained the patrol that I saw in that book.

247
00:12:31,800 --> 00:12:33,800
Ultimately, what I concluded is this:

248
00:12:33,800 --> 00:12:35,800
It's important to have vision,

249
00:12:35,800 --> 00:12:38,800
It's important to have an idealized goal,

250
00:12:38,800 --> 00:12:42,800
but that idealized goal can drive you crazy.

251
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:47,800
if you're chasing something that never actually existed,

252
00:12:47,800 --> 00:12:51,800
You know it's great,

253
00:12:51,800 --> 00:12:53,800
You have to aim at something right,

254
00:12:53,800 --> 00:12:56,800
So you might as well aim at the best iteration possible,

255
00:12:56,800 --> 00:12:59,800
but that's never actually existed,

256
00:12:59,800 --> 00:13:03,800
Maybe for a couple of months, maybe for a camping trip.

257
00:13:03,800 --> 00:13:06,800
and the other thing that I think it's important to realize

258
00:13:06,800 --> 00:13:09,800
is something I talked about in a recent podcast,

259
00:13:09,800 --> 00:13:12,800
where I draw a difference between the patrol system-

260
00:13:12,800 --> 00:13:16,800
the way that patrols are defined in the literature-

261
00:13:16,800 --> 00:13:19,800
and the patrol method, which is just making sure that

262
00:13:19,800 --> 00:13:22,800
our scouts are together in patrols,

263
00:13:22,800 --> 00:13:24,800
reaping the benefits of character development

264
00:13:24,800 --> 00:13:26,800
that comes out of being in a patrol.

265
00:13:26,800 --> 00:13:30,800
and that patrol system, the way that it's presented,

266
00:13:30,800 --> 00:13:33,800
is embedded with things that are based

267
00:13:33,800 --> 00:13:37,800
on conditions that may have once existed

268
00:13:37,800 --> 00:13:40,800
but they're not necessarily viable anymore.

269
00:13:40,800 --> 00:13:43,800
Back to the issue. we have patrols.

270
00:13:43,800 --> 00:13:46,800
They get too small if we don't recruit enough scouts.

271
00:13:46,800 --> 00:13:49,800
so we tried the quarterly reshuffle idea

272
00:13:49,800 --> 00:13:52,800
and that works fine, except for we have a few older scouts.

273
00:13:52,800 --> 00:13:54,800
They're only interested in doing their thing

274
00:13:54,800 --> 00:13:56,800
and they only want to hang out together.

275
00:13:56,800 --> 00:13:58,800
if that's who they want to hang out with,

276
00:13:58,800 --> 00:14:02,800
Let's just put them in the same patrol then,

277
00:14:02,800 --> 00:14:04,800
and put them in the same patrol,

278
00:14:04,800 --> 00:14:06,800
And I know that the answer I would get back

279
00:14:06,800 --> 00:14:08,800
is something along the lines of:

280
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:12,800
no, we need the older guys in, with the younger guys in patrols.

281
00:14:12,800 --> 00:14:13,800
Well, it's not working.

282
00:14:13,800 --> 00:14:16,800
Some of this is a structural problem.

283
00:14:16,800 --> 00:14:18,800
We create a real problem with older scouts

284
00:14:18,800 --> 00:14:22,800
by letting them remain in a scout troop until they reach 18

285
00:14:22,800 --> 00:14:25,800
and not really significantly changing their status

286
00:14:25,800 --> 00:14:27,800
since they joined when they were 11..

287
00:14:27,800 --> 00:14:29,800
In most of the rest of the world,

288
00:14:29,800 --> 00:14:32,800
you must leave a scout troop by age 14

289
00:14:32,800 --> 00:14:35,800
and move on to the next age division, which is Ventures.

290
00:14:35,800 --> 00:14:37,800
And if you think about this,

291
00:14:37,800 --> 00:14:39,800
what happens when you turn 14??

292
00:14:39,800 --> 00:14:43,800
Well, usually you leave intermediate or middle school

293
00:14:43,800 --> 00:14:45,800
and you move up to the high school.

294
00:14:45,800 --> 00:14:47,800
Why did we decide that this was a good idea

295
00:14:47,800 --> 00:14:48,800
in the school system?

296
00:14:48,800 --> 00:14:51,800
Well, there's some significant differences about you

297
00:14:51,800 --> 00:14:53,800
between the ages of 14 to 18

298
00:14:53,800 --> 00:14:56,800
than children who are 11 to 13..

299
00:14:56,800 --> 00:14:59,800
Now, for whatever reason where we don't end up

300
00:14:59,800 --> 00:15:02,800
having an official recognition of that difference,

301
00:15:02,800 --> 00:15:05,800
I work my way through that by creating one.

302
00:15:05,800 --> 00:15:07,800
We have a Venture crew-

303
00:15:07,800 --> 00:15:09,800
and I'm not going to go into all the why's and wherefor's

304
00:15:09,800 --> 00:15:12,800
and how this works from a paperwork standpoint.

305
00:15:12,800 --> 00:15:15,800
And when you reach the age of 14

306
00:15:15,800 --> 00:15:19,800
or enter high school, whichever comes first,

307
00:15:19,800 --> 00:15:21,800
you're a member of the Venture crew.

308
00:15:21,800 --> 00:15:24,800
It's a change in status that the older scouts appreciate.

309
00:15:24,800 --> 00:15:27,800
Now, to complicate things further and to confuse you further,

310
00:15:27,800 --> 00:15:30,800
if you're in the Venture crew, you might end up in a patrol.

311
00:15:30,800 --> 00:15:31,800
It all depends.

312
00:15:31,800 --> 00:15:33,800
If that's what you want to do, that's fine.

313
00:15:33,800 --> 00:15:35,800
If you want to be in the Venture crew,

314
00:15:35,800 --> 00:15:38,800
which actually functions as a patrol within the troop,

315
00:15:38,800 --> 00:15:39,800
that's fine too.

316
00:15:39,800 --> 00:15:42,800
But I'm not going to take all of the scouts

317
00:15:42,800 --> 00:15:44,800
who are 14 and up

318
00:15:44,800 --> 00:15:46,800
and scatter them through patrols,

319
00:15:46,800 --> 00:15:48,800
because that seems like a great administrative idea,

320
00:15:48,800 --> 00:15:50,800
because it doesn't work,

321
00:15:50,800 --> 00:15:53,800
because what will happen is exactly what's happening.

322
00:15:53,800 --> 00:15:54,800
They will matriculate,

323
00:15:54,800 --> 00:15:57,800
They will find each other in every situation

324
00:15:57,800 --> 00:15:58,800
and they will hang out together

325
00:15:58,800 --> 00:16:00,800
because that's what they want.

326
00:16:00,800 --> 00:16:03,800
Well, my gosh, how do the patrols of younger scouts function?

327
00:16:03,800 --> 00:16:04,800
They function fine,

328
00:16:04,800 --> 00:16:07,800
And there are plenty of older scouts in the Venture crew

329
00:16:07,800 --> 00:16:10,800
who are perfectly happy to help them over

330
00:16:10,800 --> 00:16:14,800
whatever obstacle they need to get over at a given time.

331
00:16:14,800 --> 00:16:17,800
They're perfectly willing to help them on a camp out.

332
00:16:17,800 --> 00:16:19,800
They're perfectly willing to help them with anything.

333
00:16:19,800 --> 00:16:22,800
As long as they don't have to be stuck in that patrol,

334
00:16:22,800 --> 00:16:23,800
they're fine,

335
00:16:23,800 --> 00:16:25,800
And there's always a few scouts

336
00:16:25,800 --> 00:16:28,800
who are very happy to be members of that younger scout patrol.

337
00:16:28,800 --> 00:16:30,800
So there are ways, and there are ways here,

338
00:16:30,800 --> 00:16:32,800
And what really works

339
00:16:32,800 --> 00:16:35,800
is when you observe the scouts that are there with you

340
00:16:35,800 --> 00:16:37,800
and see how it's going to work for them

341
00:16:37,800 --> 00:16:39,800
and use the patrol method

342
00:16:39,800 --> 00:16:43,800
rather than trying to adhere to the systemic approach.

343
00:16:43,800 --> 00:16:46,800
It can often be kind of messy and chaotic.

344
00:16:46,800 --> 00:16:48,800
The rate of change

345
00:16:48,800 --> 00:16:50,800
and the way things get reshuffled

346
00:16:50,800 --> 00:16:53,800
may not be optimal in your mind,

347
00:16:53,800 --> 00:16:56,800
But what's really important is that scouts

348
00:16:56,800 --> 00:17:00,800
are obtaining the benefit of being a member of a patrol.

349
00:17:00,800 --> 00:17:02,800
That's really all that you need to worry about.

350
00:17:02,800 --> 00:17:06,800
Heard from Rob Kerrigan with Troop 1 in Portland Maine,

351
00:17:06,800 --> 00:17:09,800
who said, as always, I'm enjoying the podcasts-

352
00:17:09,800 --> 00:17:12,800
Things have been going well with Troop 1..

353
00:17:12,800 --> 00:17:15,800
The new scout master and I were brainstorming

354
00:17:15,800 --> 00:17:18,800
And we were thinking we should present

355
00:17:18,800 --> 00:17:20,800
to the Patrol Leaders Council

356
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:25,800
the idea that patrols can have their own camping weekends,

357
00:17:25,800 --> 00:17:27,800
separate from everybody else,

358
00:17:27,800 --> 00:17:29,800
And we would further suggest

359
00:17:29,800 --> 00:17:32,800
that we would give each of the three patrols

360
00:17:32,800 --> 00:17:36,800
we have the choice of four predetermined camping places

361
00:17:36,800 --> 00:17:40,800
And we would give them a predetermined list of activities

362
00:17:40,800 --> 00:17:42,800
they could participate in.

363
00:17:42,800 --> 00:17:44,800
And so I'm writing to ask

364
00:17:44,800 --> 00:17:46,800
if you think it's a good idea

365
00:17:46,800 --> 00:17:49,800
for adults to establish that kind of framework

366
00:17:49,800 --> 00:17:53,800
or it undermines the idea of youth leadership.

367
00:17:53,800 --> 00:17:56,800
Well, Rob, I think I understand what you'd like to see

368
00:17:56,800 --> 00:17:58,800
and the method you suggest is not in and of itself

369
00:17:58,800 --> 00:18:00,800
a bad idea.

370
00:18:00,800 --> 00:18:01,800
It may even work.

371
00:18:01,800 --> 00:18:02,800
Your question, Rob,

372
00:18:02,800 --> 00:18:05,800
is complimentary to Bessa's question about patrols,

373
00:18:05,800 --> 00:18:08,800
because I sometimes wonder, looking at my Troop,

374
00:18:08,800 --> 00:18:10,800
if we have any patrols at all

375
00:18:10,800 --> 00:18:13,800
other than mealtimes and some isolated activities.

376
00:18:13,800 --> 00:18:15,800
I mean, when I encourage,

377
00:18:15,800 --> 00:18:19,800
when I have attempted to encourage patrol spirit

378
00:18:19,800 --> 00:18:21,800
in a traditional way,

379
00:18:21,800 --> 00:18:24,800
trying to heighten the separation between patrols,

380
00:18:24,800 --> 00:18:27,800
my scouts kind of look at me and wonder what I'm saying.

381
00:18:27,800 --> 00:18:30,800
I like to call it the dog with a new pan. look,

382
00:18:30,800 --> 00:18:34,800
They just have no idea what I'm trying to achieve.

383
00:18:34,800 --> 00:18:36,800
And I've talked with my older scouts about it

384
00:18:36,800 --> 00:18:38,800
and they look at me and they say:

385
00:18:38,800 --> 00:18:41,800
you know, that sounds kind of divisive

386
00:18:41,800 --> 00:18:43,800
and exclusive.

387
00:18:43,800 --> 00:18:45,800
That was the sense of what they told me.

388
00:18:45,800 --> 00:18:48,800
They kind of like being in a big giant group.

389
00:18:48,800 --> 00:18:50,800
They like their patrols

390
00:18:50,800 --> 00:18:53,800
and they flow back and forth between

391
00:18:53,800 --> 00:18:55,800
being identified with a patrol

392
00:18:55,800 --> 00:18:57,800
and everybody being in the troop.

393
00:18:57,800 --> 00:18:59,800
So they're not being willful about

394
00:18:59,800 --> 00:19:01,800
not being with their patrol.

395
00:19:01,800 --> 00:19:05,800
It's just that they have a concept of group

396
00:19:05,800 --> 00:19:07,800
that is maybe a little bit different

397
00:19:07,800 --> 00:19:10,800
than what I have been led to understand

398
00:19:10,800 --> 00:19:12,800
it ought to be.

399
00:19:12,800 --> 00:19:14,800
I again invoke the idea

400
00:19:14,800 --> 00:19:17,800
of the difference between patrol system and method.

401
00:19:17,800 --> 00:19:19,800
I mean the system says this is how it ought to work.

402
00:19:19,800 --> 00:19:22,800
The method might be a little bit different.

403
00:19:22,800 --> 00:19:24,800
The point of the patrol method is being responsible

404
00:19:24,800 --> 00:19:25,800
for one another.

405
00:19:25,800 --> 00:19:27,800
That's a core value of it, right?

406
00:19:27,800 --> 00:19:29,800
The idea of patrol spirit

407
00:19:29,800 --> 00:19:31,800
and brighter lines between the patrols

408
00:19:31,800 --> 00:19:34,800
may appeal to our scouts

409
00:19:34,800 --> 00:19:35,800
and it may not,

410
00:19:35,800 --> 00:19:36,800
But, that being said,

411
00:19:36,800 --> 00:19:38,800
there's still plenty of opportunities for patrols

412
00:19:38,800 --> 00:19:40,800
to work on a level that serves

413
00:19:40,800 --> 00:19:42,800
the purpose of character development

414
00:19:42,800 --> 00:19:45,800
and the idea that Rob has come up with here.

415
00:19:45,800 --> 00:19:47,800
yeah, it could work,

416
00:19:47,800 --> 00:19:51,800
And I don't think you creating a framework

417
00:19:51,800 --> 00:19:54,800
is, or defining the choices

418
00:19:54,800 --> 00:19:57,800
is, in any way usurping. you know

419
00:19:57,800 --> 00:19:59,800
the youth leadership

420
00:19:59,800 --> 00:20:01,800
I mentioned somewhere in this podcast.

421
00:20:01,800 --> 00:20:03,800
you know we have a specific field to play,

422
00:20:03,800 --> 00:20:05,800
so most of the choices are made anyway.

423
00:20:05,800 --> 00:20:08,800
So, to kind of put a bow on this whole thing, right,

424
00:20:08,800 --> 00:20:10,800
When we're talking about patrols

425
00:20:10,800 --> 00:20:12,800
and we're talking about the way patrols operate

426
00:20:12,800 --> 00:20:14,800
and we're talking about what the benefits

427
00:20:14,800 --> 00:20:18,800
of character development are from the patrol method, right,

428
00:20:18,800 --> 00:20:20,800
Well, we have to.

429
00:20:20,800 --> 00:20:24,800
it's probably worth observing your scouts

430
00:20:24,800 --> 00:20:28,800
and then talking to them about these questions

431
00:20:28,800 --> 00:20:30,800
and seeing what they're deriving

432
00:20:30,800 --> 00:20:32,800
from the way that they're managing things.

433
00:20:32,800 --> 00:20:36,800
My experience has been over the past 10 or 12 years is:

434
00:20:36,800 --> 00:20:38,800
I've watched scouts,

435
00:20:38,800 --> 00:20:40,800
I've watched the group size affinity

436
00:20:40,800 --> 00:20:42,800
kind of shift a little bit.

437
00:20:42,800 --> 00:20:45,800
That doesn't make me want to abandon the patrol method.

438
00:20:45,800 --> 00:20:48,800
It just says that it would be a good idea

439
00:20:48,800 --> 00:20:50,800
for us to be responsive

440
00:20:50,800 --> 00:20:52,800
to whatever shift is going on-

441
00:20:52,800 --> 00:20:54,800
if indeed there is a shift going on-

442
00:20:54,800 --> 00:20:56,800
and keep our eyes on the ball,

443
00:20:56,800 --> 00:20:58,800
which is the character development opportunities

444
00:20:58,800 --> 00:21:00,800
that you get from being in a patrol.

445
00:21:00,800 --> 00:21:02,800
So those email messages.

446
00:21:02,800 --> 00:21:05,800
well, I think that will be enough for today.

447
00:21:05,800 --> 00:21:07,800
I would love to hear from you.

448
00:21:07,800 --> 00:21:10,800
I'd like to answer your questions here on the podcast.

